          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I keep getting urine infections.",
          "answer": "Recurrent urine infections are worth raising with a clinician, who can check whether a test or further assessment is needed. The safety check found no emergency pattern in your message. That does not rule out a serious problem, so seek medical advice promptly if you develop fever, back or side pain, blood in your urine, confusion, or are unable to keep fluids down.\n\nThe reference material I found describes some factors that can make urine infections more likely. These include female anatomy with a shorter urethra, sexual activity, certain contraceptives including spermicides and diaphragms, incomplete bladder emptying, kidney stones, diabetes, and previous infections (*Urinary tract infection*, General clinical reference). It also says that in people with recurrent infection, a urine culture, a lab test that identifies the bacteria, is one of the cases where testing can change management (*Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: treatment, culture indications and recurrence*, Infectious diseases and urology society guidance).\n\nThe sources I found do not explain how to prevent recurrent infections, and they do not say which factors apply to you. A clinician can look at your history and tell you which of these, if any, are relevant and what testing or next steps make sense.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
