          "turn_index": 2,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do I need an antibiotic every time?",
          "answer": "The sources I found do not say whether you need an antibiotic every time you have a urine infection. The most relevant passage is from *Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: treatment, culture indications and recurrence*. It says that when someone has typical symptoms and no vaginal discharge or irritation, empiric treatment (starting treatment based on symptoms, without waiting for a culture) is appropriate. It also says a urine dipstick adds little in that group. That passage describes when treatment starts. It does not cover whether treatment is always needed or how antibiotic use over time should be managed.\n\nThe antibiotic stewardship guidance I found is about upper respiratory infections such as colds and sore throats. It does not cover urine infections, so I can't use it to answer your question.\n\nA clinician can tell you whether antibiotics are right for a particular episode. They can also explain what to do if your symptoms are unclear. Please seek medical advice promptly if you develop fever, back or side pain, blood in your urine, confusion, or are unable to keep fluids down.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
